<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#casta">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Casta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Casta, Spanish and Portuguese for 'lineage', was a label of descent and standing in colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>, chiefly for people of mixed Spanish, Indigenous and African ancestry. The basic mixed categories in official documents were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizo</a>, generally the child of a Spaniard and an Indigenous person, and mulatto, of a Spaniard and a black African. Spaniards and mestizos paid no tribute and could be ordained; free black people, Indigenous people and other castas paid tribute and were barred from the priesthood. Historians now question whether a rigid '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system' existed: the casta paintings of the 18th century, which set many mixtures out in a fixed order under labels rarely used in practice, may have been an attempt to order a more fluid reality.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/casta.jpg" alt="Casta" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casta_painting_all.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Casta is an Iberian word, found in Spanish, Portuguese and other Iberian languages since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>; it is first recorded in Spanish in 1417, and the Portuguese casta gave English the word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> in the early modern period. Its colonial use grew out of limpieza de sangre, purity of blood, an idea that developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> after the reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a> and spread through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a>, holding that new converts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> were prone to heresy and prompting genealogical inquiries to exclude families that had not been Christian.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> checked the Old Christian standing of its officials in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and by the end of the 1500s some records listed categories such as black and mulatto, and sometimes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizo</a> and indio. From the late 16th century some inquiries into ancestry treated any link with black Africans, and sometimes with Indians, as a stain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The categories described a person's calidad, or quality, which combined legal status as a tributary liable to a head tax with culture and race. Placement was flexible: people could pass as another group to hold offices reserved for whites, or change labels to match a spouse's. In 1617 Christóbal de Toro was described as a mulato although white, showing that the term could mark calidad rather than appearance.</p><p>The crown divided the population into the República de Indios and the República de Españoles, the latter taking in Spaniards, black people and the mixed castas. From the late 16th century royal decrees sought to keep black and Indian people apart; black people were forbidden to live with Indians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 1580, in Chile in 1586 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in 1587, though repeated orders suggest the decrees often failed. Laws from 1577 eased the collection of taxes from the castas and barred black and mulatto people from carrying weapons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>During the Mexican War of Independence José María Morelos called for ending the formal distinctions between racial groups, calling them one and all Americans, and casta designations were abolished at independence in 1821. The idea of a colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system was developed in the 1940s by Ángel Rosenblat and Gonzalo Aguirre Beltrán; later studies by Pilar Gonzalbo, Joanne Rappaport, Berta Ares and Ben Vinson have challenged it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Casta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A layered society emerged of Spaniards born in Spain, American-born criollos, indigenous people, Africans and mixed groups classified as <b>castas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Spanish America colonial law and ideology ranked people by descent and colour, and the <b>casta</b> paintings of the 18th century show a neat ladder of mixtures.</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>casta</b> paintings of the 18th century show a neat ladder of mixtures, but many historians now see a looser order, in which a person’s standing (calidad) turned on wealth, occupation and reputation as well as birth, and could change from one record to the next.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li></ul></section>
